.\" Manpage for sysrepo-notifd.
.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii sysrepo-notifd.8
.\"

.TH SYSREPO-NOTIFD 8 "2026-08-07" "sysrepo"
.SH NAME
sysrepo-notifd \- sysrepo notification daemon
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sysrepo-notifd
[\fB\-h\fP] [\fB\-v\fP \fILEVEL\fP] [\fB\-d\fP] [\fB\-p\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-s\fP \fIDIR\fP]
.br
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBsysrepo-notifd\fP is a daemon implementing RFC 8639 configured subscriptions.
It reads the \fIietf-subscribed-notifications\fP configuration and manages the
lifecycle of subscriptions and their receivers, delivering notifications from
filterable event streams over configurable transports (UDP out of the box).
On startup it requires these three modules to be installed:
\fIietf-subscribed-notifications\fP, \fIietf-subscribed-notif-receivers\fP
and \fIietf-udp-notif-transport\fP.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR "\-h\fR,\fP \-\^\-help"
Prints usage help.
.TP
.BR "\-V\fR,\fP \-\^\-version"
Prints only information about sysrepo version.
.TP
.BR "\-v\fR,\fP \-\^\-verbosity \fILEVEL\fP"
Change verbosity to a level. \fILEVEL\fP can be a string or a number:
 \[bu] \fBnone\fP/\fB0\fP
 \[bu] \fBerror\fP/\fB1\fP (default)
 \[bu] \fBwarning\fP/\fB2\fP
 \[bu] \fBinfo\fP/\fB3\fP
 \[bu] \fBverbose\fP/\fB4\fP
 \[bu] \fBdebug\fP/\fB5\fP
.TP
.BR "\-d\fR,\fP \-\^\-debug"
Debug mode - is not daemonized and logs to stderr instead of syslog.
.TP
.BR "\-p\fR,\fP \-\^\-pid\-file \fIPATH\fP"
Create a PID file at the specified path with the PID written only once
initialization is finished.
.TP
.BR "\-s\fR,\fP \-\^\-schema\-dir \fIDIR\fP"
Set the directory with internal sysrepo YANG modules.
.
.SH ACCESS CONTROL
The daemon requires NACM (RFC 8341) to be configured and will fail to
start if NACM cannot be initialized. NACM is used to apply per-receiver
access control:
.
.TP
.B Receiver nacm-user leaf
The
.I sysrepo-subscribed-notif
module augments
.I ietf-subscribed-notif-receivers
with a mandatory
.I nacm-user
leaf on each
.IR receiver-instance .
The daemon creates a dedicated sysrepo session bound to that
NACM identity for the receiver's subscription. Notifications the user is
not authorized to read are silently excluded (the
.I excluded-event-records
counter is incremented). The leaf is transport-agnostic and applies to
all configured transports.
.
.TP
.B Write access to the nacm-user leaf
The
.I nacm-user
leaf is annotated with
.IR nacm:default-deny-write ,
so standard NACM write-rules (RFC 8341) govern who may set it: only the
recovery user or users with an explicit permit rule. Both NACM
configuration groups
.RI ( ietf-netconf-acm:nacm/groups )
and Unix system groups (when
.I enable-external-groups
is
.RI true ,
the default) are evaluated by sysrepo automatically. Direct sysrepo API
clients (e.g.
.IR sysrepocfg )
that do not have a NACM user set bypass these checks; this relies on OS
file permissions on the sysrepo shared memory and repository directories
to prevent unauthorized access.
.
.TP
.B NACM statistics
The daemon subscribes to the NACM global statistics
.RI ( denied-operations ,
.IR denied-data-writes ,
.IR denied-notifications )
so that the standard RFC 8341 counters are populated and accessible via
the
.I ietf-netconf-acm
data tree. If another process on the system is already providing these
statistics (e.g. a running NETCONF/RESTCONF server), the daemon's
subscription silently becomes a no-op and the counters are provided by
that other process.
.
.TP
.B Future: DTLS
Plain UDP is scoped to controlled environments (RFC 8085 §3.6, RFC 8799).
DTLS is the future cryptographically-bound transport for
.IR sysrepo-notifd ;
until then, the
.I nacm-user
leaf provides configuration-based access control over plain UDP.
.
